Add GET/POST /api/settings to the Wi-Fi variants' local trusted-LAN
HTTP server, reusing the existing Aliro settings parser, value
limits, NVS store, Matter AutoRelockTime update, and rollback
path. Applied only to nanoc6-wifi and atoms3-lite-wifi via
variants.json; nanoc6-thread never applies, compiles, links, or
serves any of the new code.

Scope:
  * MODIFY main/aliro_settings.h: export
      enum class AliroSettingsApplyOutcome
          { kOk, kBadRequest, kUnknownKey, kInvalidValue,
            kStorage, kMatter, kBusy };
      AliroSettingsApplyOutcome
          AliroSettingsSerializedSetApply(char *set_line);
    The outcome enum lets callers surface each specific parser
    rejection alongside NVS or Matter failures. The declared
    entry point handles the read-current + parse + apply as one
    serialized transaction under an internal apply mutex.
  * MODIFY main/aliro_settings.cpp: add a second FreeRTOS mutex
    g_apply_mutex that serializes concurrent applies from the
    serial protocol task and the HTTP settings handler
    (g_settings_mutex remains the fast getter lock).
    AliroSettingsSerializedSetApply checks the xSemaphoreTake
    result, re-reads current UNDER the lock, calls AliroParse
    Request against the fresh baseline, then hands the resulting
    candidate to the existing ApplySettings (unchanged: same NVS
    write, Matter attribute update, and rollback). The detailed
    ESP_LOGE with esp_err_to_name(error) survives inside the
    shared apply path so console debugging keeps its original
    context regardless of the caller. Init/teardown handles both
    mutexes. Serial ProcessLine now dispatches GET vs SET, sending
    SET through the same serialized entry point so a concurrent
    HTTP + serial partial apply cannot lose either request's
    updates. Serial error codes are unchanged.
  * MODIFY main/aliro_local_web.cpp: add GET /api/settings and
    POST /api/settings. Both emit the seven values as JSON with
    lowercase six-hex RGB. POST accepts a bounded
    application/x-www-form-urlencoded body (256 bytes max);
    declared body length is read as size_t (never narrowed to
    int) so a very large content_len cannot wrap before the
    over-limit check. Short reads and receive timeouts retry
    with a bounded cap. The body is validated against a strict
    allowlist ([a-zA-Z0-9_] plus '=' and '&') and rewritten into
    an "ALIRO/1 SET k=v ..." protocol line, then handed to
    AliroSettingsSerializedSetApply so the read + parse + apply
    happen atomically. Every rejection path surfaces a JSON
    error body under the right HTTP status. Status strings are
    literals (esp_http_server's httpd_err_code_t enum has no 415
    value in ESP-IDF v5.5, so send_json_error takes a literal
    status line). httpd_config max_uri_handlers bumped from 4 to
    6; the two new register calls ride the same atomic URI-
    registration failure branch that already retains s_server
    when httpd_stop also fails.

Explicit non-goals for this patch: no settings form / page UI, no
logs, no OTA, no reset, no reboot, no authentication, no
redaction, no cJSON or new runtime dependency, no partition
change, no second settings store, no duplicated validation.

